well you look if you came to me and  offered to pay me eggs what I'm making  now and told me that I could stay home  I'm gonna certainly think about it hi  I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and  today we're talking with Michael Tanner  he's a senior fellow at the Cato  Institute and he's the author of work  versus welfare tradeoff 2013 Michael  thanks for talking to us pleasure to be  with you okay this is a fascinating  study that's generated a lot of feedback  in commentary and analysis essentially  what you're doing is updating a 1995  report where you looked at the benefits  of welfare programs versus the benefits  of low-wage work what do you find well  what we found out was that very little  has actually changed since 1995 that in  many ways welfare still pays better than  work at least in a great many state okay  yeah and you have you found that in 18  states welfare does pay less in in  adjusted inflation adjusted terms than  in 1995 but also that welfare pays more  than the minimum wage and something like  35 states welfare pays more than minimum  wage in 35 states it pays better than  $15 an hour and 13 states and in the  eight most generous states it actually  pays better than a $20 an hour job is  there a correlation where welfare pays  more that you see fewer people in the  workplace or higher unemployment rates  things like that we weren't able to  actually match unemployment rates or  welfare participation rates with the  level of wages well we were able to find  though is that those states that tended  to have very generous benefit packages  had relatively few people on welfare  participating in their welfare to work  programs okay yeah and this was part of  the backdrop of this is that in the  welfare reform that was done in the late  90s after 95 that was the linchpin right  that people had to work in order to get  welfare but you're finding that that's  that impetus has kind of faded over time  but many ways that was the biggest  surprise to me in doing this study  because I had been pretty much convinced  that as part of the reforms that Clinton  in the Republicans did in 96 they'll be  moving everybody from welfare to work  and that if you were going to get  benefits you were going to have to work  from now on right and we did see in the  90s and the late night here in the wake  of that mass  of decreases in welfare caseloads  caseloads went down and interestingly  poverty rates went down child poverty  went down black child poverty went down  but what we found now is that only  nationwide only about 42% of people in  welfare were actually participating in  work and work was pretty broadly defined  there that includes job search which is  looking for work or job training  programs going to college counts as work  and only about one in five was actually  in a non subsidized job so we really you  know aren't moving people from welfare  to work anymore somewhere in the study  you say you know that uh it's been noted  that the you know the highest marginal  tax rate paid by people is actually  people who leave welfare and go to work  and lose their benefits could you  explain what you mean by that I'm sure  what welfare benefits are not taxable  but wages are if you were earning a  dollar on welfare and you left welfare  and took a job and earned a dollar and  wages you would have to pay taxes on  that don't forget this the payroll tax  starts at the first dollar you earned  there's no exemptions no deductions  anything like that so that's a huge  marginal tax rate when you leave welfare  for work can you give us a kind of  estimate of you know or an average of  how much it does the welfare  compensation package what is the  equivalent in a wage well it really  varies a great deal from state to state  in the state like Mississippi which had  the lowest it was around sixteen  thousand a year and change which isn't  that great but in Hawaii you'd have to  have a job that learned as much as sixty  thousand dollars a year to break even so  it's a pretty broad spread right and and  essentially the argument that you're  making here is that if you have to make  a job if you have to make sixty thousand  dollars in order to get the equivalent  of what you might be able to pull in on  a welfare program you know it you know  Max Weber couldn't come up with  Protestant or Catholic people that are  going to do that right well then you  look if you came to me and offered to  pay me eggs what I'm making now and tell  me that I could stay home I'm going to  certainly think about it because work is  actually a sacrifice I mean when you go  to work there's financial sacrifices  involved childcare transportation the  clothes you have to buy to go to work  things like that and there's lots of  other things too there's loss of leisure  time if you're a mother with small  children you're not gonna get to be home  and see  children grow up the same way and work  is hard so you have to take that  sacrifice into account and balance that  against the possibility of income and  advancement and things like that it  begins to get tougher when you have the  thrill welfare in the mix what we're  what you're basically saying is that you  know work is hard and if you can get  something close to that without working  a lot of people are gonna take that sure  people on welfare there's no evidence  suggest there any lazier than the rest  of us but they're also not dumber than  the rest of us and if you offer them the  same incentives that's what they'll do  well we suggest a couple of things in  the short-term  number one is we really need to look at  those work requirements again and go  back to the idea that if you're going to  collect welfare benefits then you must  work the idea that you can have a state  like Massachusetts and only 18 percent  of welfare recipients are even in the  broad definition of work that's just a  disgrace we need to deal with that and  second we need to consolidate all these  different welfare programs in some way  and begin to put a cap on it  just recently Britain capped its welfare  payments at five hundred pounds a week  you know we're starting to fall behind  the Europeans when it comes to welfare  reform that suggests you've node we need  to rethink this speaking of foreign  countries one of the critiques I saw of  this study it was not actually about the  methodology or the conclusions but it  said look if we have these generous  welfare programs you know Cato is a an  open borders libertarian outfit you know  reason also big on open borders how do  you respond to the criticism that yeah  you've documented exactly why we cannot  allow immigrants into the country well a  couple things on that first of all the  Cato is actually advocated that we wall  off the welfare state rather than  walling off the country we've suggested  let's cut off benefits for welfare to  the new immigrants we still think  they're gonna flock to this country  because we're still the land of  opportunity the second part of that is  though is that welfare recipients or  I've sorry the immigrants actually use  welfare benefits at a lower weight the  native-born Americans they're not coming  here for welfare they're coming here to  work and second they actually add to the  prosperity and economic growth in a  country if you look to states that are  doing really well in terms of economic  growth they tend to be states that have  high immigration it's basically then  they come to this country they prove the  economic conditions for everybody  raising wages for everybody alright well  we'll leave it there I want to thank  Michael tanner senior fellow at the Cato  in  Institute and the author most recently  work versus welfare trade-off thanks for  talking to Reason TV my pleasure

